Now plan. Could define a shared class `.pathland-control` for native form controls. But date/color are `<input>`; select is `<select>`; textarea/inputs already `.pathland-input`. We can extend `.pathland-input` selector to include `.pathland-select` etc. Maybe simplest:
- Add `.pathland-select`, `.pathland-date`, `.pathland-color` classes and CSS grouping with `.pathland-input`.
- Add `class="pathland-progress"` to `<progress>` and CSS with `appearance:none`, tokenized track/fill.
- Add `class="pathland-video"` to `<video>` and CSS for background/border/radius; note native controls remain UA-drawn.
- Add `class="pathland-control"` to date/color/select? Or reuse `.pathland-input`. Since these are text-like controls, apply `pathland-input` to select/date/color too. But `.pathland-input` has `width:100%; flex:1 1 auto;` which may not suit date/color/select inside a stack? Actually text field wrapper handles. Applying `.pathland-input` directly to select gives width 100% and flex 1, likely okay for standalone. But for `PICKER` within a stack, it would fill main axis? The inline STYLE may include width/height; `width:100%` could override. Better a base class that does not force width/flex. Introduce `.pathland-control` for shared visual tokens; `.pathland-input` extends it for fields:
```
.pathland-control, .pathland-input { ... visual tokens ... }
.pathland-input { display:block; width:100%; flex:1 1 auto; min-width:0; padding... }
.pathland-control { display:inline-flex; align-items:center; min-height: ...; }
```
But `.pathland-input` already sets visual; refactor to avoid duplication:
```
.pathland-control, .pathland-input {
  border-radius: var(--pl-input-radius);
  background-color: var(--pl-input-background);
  color: var(--pl-input-foreground);
  font-size: var(--pl-input-font-size);
  font-family: inherit;
  outline: 1px solid var(--pl-input-border);
  outline-offset: -1px;
  ...
}
```
